---
import { Code } from 'astro-expressive-code/components';
import Hero from '../components/hero.astro';
import ThemeToggle from '../components/theme-toggle.astro';
import Demo from '../components/demo.astro';
import Layout from '../layouts/Landing.astro';
const dependency = 'implementation("com.alexstyl:prosakt:0.1.0")';
const usage = `import com.alexstyl.prosakt.ktFile

val source = ktFile {
    ktValue("message") {
        initializer { literal("Hello from Prosa.kt") }
    }
}`;
---
<Layout>
  <Hero />
  <Demo />

  <section class="section benefits" aria-labelledby="benefits-title">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Built into the DSL</p>
      <h2 id="benefits-title">Focus on what to generate.</h2>
      <p>Use Kotlin builders to define declarations and expressions. Prosa.kt turns them into source text
        with imports, escaped literals, and consistent formatting.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="benefit-grid">
      <article>
        <span class="section-number" aria-hidden="true">01</span>
        <h3>Automatic imports</h3>
        <p>Reference a qualified type or function. Prosa.kt collects, sorts, and deduplicates its imports,
          and uses qualified names when symbols collide.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href="/docs/types/">See how imports work <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="section-number" aria-hidden="true">02</span>
        <h3>Escaped literals</h3>
        <p>Pass strings and numbers to <code>literal()</code>. Quotes, backslashes, and dollar signs
          are escaped for the generated Kotlin.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href="/docs/expressions/">Explore expressions <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="section-number" aria-hidden="true">03</span>
        <h3>Consistent formatting</h3>
        <p>Get consistent indentation and automatic line wrapping for structured expressions.
          Keep generated source readable when reviewing or debugging it.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href="/docs/files/">See the output rules <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section installation" id="installation" aria-labelledby="installation-title">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Getting started</p>
      <h2 id="installation-title">Generate your first<br />Kotlin file.</h2>
      <p>Add Prosa.kt to your JVM project or Kotlin Multiplatform source set, then call <code>ktFile</code> to generate source.</p>
      <a class="text-link" href="/docs/installation/">Full installation guide <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
    </div>
    <div class="install-steps">
      <div>
        <h3><span class="section-number">01</span> Add the dependency</h3>
        <p>With <code>mavenCentral()</code> configured, add this to your dependencies.</p>
        <Code code={dependency} lang="kotlin" title="build.gradle.kts" />
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3><span class="section-number">02</span> Define a file with the DSL</h3>
        <Code code={usage} lang="kotlin" title="Your generator" />
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3><span class="section-number">03</span> Save the generated source</h3>
        <p><code>ktFile</code> returns a string. Save it to a <code>.kt</code> file or pass it to your existing generation pipeline.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href="/docs/first-file/">Walk through your first file <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section questions" aria-labelledby="questions-title">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Using Prosa.kt</p>
      <h2 id="questions-title">Common questions.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="question-list">
      <details>
        <summary>Which platforms are supported?</summary>
        <p>All of them. Prosa.kt is written in pure Kotlin and targets JVM, JavaScript, WebAssembly, and Kotlin/Native.
          Add it to the source set that runs your generator. <a href="/docs/installation/">See the setup for your project.</a></p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Does it compile the generated code?</summary>
        <p>Prosa.kt returns Kotlin source text. Your project handles writing files, registering generated sources,
          and compilation. The DSL checks structural mistakes, but it does not type-check the generated program.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Can I use my own loops and conditions?</summary>
        <p>Yes. In your Kotlin project, the builder is ordinary Kotlin: use loops, conditions, and your own functions
          to drive generation. This browser playground supports a smaller set of builder calls and literal values.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What license does Prosa.kt use?</summary>
        <p>Prosa.kt is open source under the MIT license. You can use it in personal and commercial projects.
          <a href="https://github.com/alexstyl/ProsaKt">Browse the source on GitHub.</a></p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="final-cta" aria-labelledby="final-title">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Add Prosa.kt to your project</p>
      <h2 id="final-title">Start generating Kotlin.</h2>
      <p>Follow the setup guide and generate your first Kotlin file.</p>
    </div>
    <a class="button primary" href="/docs/installation/">Get started <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
  </section>

  <footer class="site-footer">
    <a class="wordmark" href="#main">Prosa.kt</a>
    <p>Built by <a href="https://alexstyl.com">Alex Styl</a>. Open source, MIT licensed.</p>
    <nav aria-label="Footer navigation">
      <a href="/docs/installation/">Docs</a>
      <a href="https://github.com/alexstyl/ProsaKt">GitHub</a>
      <a href="/llms.txt">llms.txt</a>
    </nav>
    <ThemeToggle />
  </footer>
</Layout>
